    "available_at": "2026-11-16T23:10:07.155585+00:00",
    "body": "A view of the area surrounding the former Panguna copper-gold mine in Papua New Guinea. Credit: Rio Tinto.\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nRio Tinto (LSE: RIO; NYSE: RIO; ASX: RIO) has pledged $250 million (C$356 million) to address environmental and human-rights impacts from the former Panguna copper-gold mine in Bougainville, Papua New Guinea, a decade after relinquishing its stake in the operator.\nThe contribution will be paid over coming years into a fund agreed with Bougainville Copper (ASX: BOC), the autonomous regional government and community leaders. Its governance and spending priorities still require consultation, the parties said Monday .\n“Today marks a very important step on that journey,” community leader and lead complainant Theonila Roka-Matbob said in the joint announcement.\nThe commitment follows years of pressure from communities living with mine waste and unsafe infrastructure after production stopped in 1989. It moves the response beyond assessing damage towards financing remedies, although the announcement does not establish that the contribution will cover all outstanding work.\nRio shares fell 1.6% to $81.60 Monday morning in New York, valuing the company at about $142 billion (C$203 billion). The stock has ranged between $68.70 and $112.58 over the past year.\nContinuing dangers\nAn independent investigation published in December 2024 identified unstable mine structures, contaminated river areas and flooding that threatened homes, food gardens and access to drinking water.\nThe Australian Human Rights Law Centre, which represented affected communities, said the investigation documented continuing dangers to lives and livelihoods. Environmental consultancy Tetra Tech Coffey conducted the two-year assessment after Rio agreed in 2021 to fund the work in response to a community complaint. The investigation made 24 recommendations and identified questions requiring further study, including possible health effects, the law centre said.\nThe risks extend beyond the former pit. Waste carried through the Jaba-Kawerong river system has affected downstream communities, while damaged infrastructure and flood-prone crossings have made access to services more difficult.\nRiver work\nThe new fund will support work in river and delta areas and social programs across affected communities. Existing projects include bridge construction, hazardous-material removal at Loloho port and water and sanitation improvements. Those commitments are separate from decisions about redeveloping the mine and port, which rest with Bougainville’s government and people, the parties said.\nPanguna operated from 1972 until an uprising and civil war forced its closure in 1989. Rio held a controlling interest in Bougainville Copper until 2016, when it transferred its shares to the Bougainville and Papua New Guinea governments without payment.\nThe long gap between closure and the current funding agreement shows how mining’s environmental and community impacts can remain unresolved long after production ends and ownership changes.\n“There is still much work ahead,” Bougainville Copper CEO Sir Mel Togolo said.\nPrint\nTAG: copper copper production environment\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold\nREGION: Australia, NZ & South Pacific\nCOMPANY: Bougainville Copper Rio Tinto\nRepublish this article",
